# ARCHITECTURE.md — SkillGap Technical Architecture

## 1. Architecture Overview

SkillGap is built as a **modular monolith**: a single Next.js application (frontend + API routes) backed by a PostgreSQL database, with clearly separated internal layers (routes → services → repositories) so components can later be split out (e.g., a background worker) without a rewrite.

**Why a modular monolith and not microservices:**
- The team is small (student/hackathon scale); microservices add operational overhead (multiple deployments, service discovery, network failure modes) with no corresponding benefit at this scale.
- All core domain objects (User, Target, Skill, Evidence, Roadmap) are tightly related and queried together (e.g., the dashboard joins across nearly every entity) — a single relational database with transactional consistency is simpler and more correct than a distributed system.
- A single deployable unit is far easier to build, test, and deploy within a hackathon/student timeline.

The one component that is architected as logically separate from request/response handling is **long-running work** (AI calls, GitHub sync): these run as asynchronous background jobs (implemented as a lightweight in-process/serverless job queue for MVP, upgradeable to a dedicated queue later — see §13) so user-facing requests stay fast.

