## 12. Security Architecture

- HTTPS everywhere (enforced at the hosting platform level).
- Input validation (Zod) at every API boundary; output encoding on the frontend to prevent XSS from user-entered text (e.g., job descriptions, resume-derived text) rendered in the UI.
- CSRF protection via Auth.js's built-in CSRF token handling for state-changing form submissions; OAuth `state` parameter validated on GitHub callback.
- File upload validation: MIME type + magic-byte check (not just file extension), size limit, virus/malware scanning out of scope for MVP but flagged as a risk (see Architecture §18).
- Secrets (AI API key, GitHub OAuth client secret, DB credentials, token-encryption key) stored only in environment variables / platform secret manager — never committed to source control.
- Rate limiting on expensive endpoints (`/analyze`, `/resume`, `/github/sync`) per user, to control cost and prevent abuse.
- Principle of least privilege for GitHub scopes; explicit, separate consent step for private repository access.

