## 13. Performance & Scalability

- **Current design target:** Hundreds to low thousands of users (student/hackathon → early-stage scale), not high-traffic production scale.
- Long-running work (AI calls, GitHub sync) is offloaded to background jobs so API request latency stays low and predictable.
- Database indexes on all frequent lookup paths (see §7) to keep dashboard aggregation queries fast as data grows.
- Caching of GitHub API responses (ETag-based conditional requests) and AI extraction results (content-hash keyed) reduces redundant external calls.
- **Scaling path if load grows:** (1) Move the DB-backed job queue to a dedicated queue (e.g., BullMQ + Redis) without changing the service-layer API; (2) introduce Redis for hot-path caching (readiness scores, dashboard aggregates); (3) split GitHub sync into a separately-scaled worker process if sync volume grows beyond what serverless functions handle well. None of these require re-architecting the core domain model.

