## 18. Technical Risks and Trade-offs

- **Trade-off — Modular monolith:** Simpler now, but background-heavy workloads (AI, GitHub sync) share compute with the web app on serverless platforms; if sync volume grows significantly, cold-start/timeout limits on serverless functions may require moving sync to a dedicated long-running worker (mitigation path documented in §13).
- **Trade-off — No RAG/vector store for MVP:** Simpler, but limits future features like "search across all my past job description analyses" without a later retrieval layer addition.
- **Risk — AI provider outage or rate limiting:** Mitigated by fallback deterministic extraction (keyword/skill-table matching) so core flows degrade rather than fully break.
- **Risk — GitHub API rate limits at scale:** Mitigated by caching, ETag conditional requests, and user-level sync cooldowns; a true production scale-up would need a dedicated sync worker with its own rate-limit budget management.
- **Risk — File upload attack surface (malicious PDFs):** MVP relies on library-level PDF parsing safety and file-type/size validation; a production hardening pass would add sandboxed parsing or a managed document-processing service.
- **Trade-off — DB-stored/short-lived-URL file storage vs. dedicated object storage:** Simpler for MVP, but object storage (e.g., Vercel Blob/S3-compatible) should be adopted early if resume volume or file size grows, since large binary blobs in the primary relational DB hurt backup/restore performance over time.
